  "content": {
    "id": "p49-q12",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "$4(k+1)x^{2}+4kx+k-2=0$. Find the values of $k$ for which this equation has the following numbers of real solutions. Use the discriminant when the equation is quadratic, and check separately when the coefficient of $x^{2}$ is zero.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p49-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "No real solutions ($\\Delta<0$ for a quadratic).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k<-2$, since $\\Delta=16(k+2)<0$.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}a&=4(k+1),\\quad b=4k,\\quad c=k-2\\\\\\Delta&=(4k)^2-4\\bigl(4(k+1)\\bigr)(k-2)\\\\\\Delta&=16k^2-16(k^2-k-2)\\\\\\Delta&=16(k+2)\\\\16(k+2)&<0\\\\k&<-2\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $k=-1$, the equation is $-4x-3=0$, which has one real solution. Hence $k<-2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "One real solution ($\\Delta=0$ for a quadratic).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k=-2$ or $k=-1$. The latter gives a linear equation.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(4k)^2-4\\bigl(4(k+1)\\bigr)(k-2)\\\\\\Delta&=16k^2-16(k^2-k-2)\\\\\\Delta&=16(k+2)\\\\16(k+2)&=0\\\\k&=-2\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $k=-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}-4x-3&=0\\\\-4x&=3\\\\x&=-\\frac34\\end{align*}$$\nThis linear equation also has exactly one real solution. Hence $k=-2$ or $k=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Two real solutions ($\\Delta>0$ for a quadratic).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k>-2$, with $k\\ne-1$, since $\\Delta>0$ and the equation must be quadratic.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(4k)^2-4\\bigl(4(k+1)\\bigr)(k-2)\\\\\\Delta&=16k^2-16(k^2-k-2)\\\\\\Delta&=16(k+2)\\\\16(k+2)&>0\\\\k&>-2\\end{align*}$$\nExclude $k=-1$: then $-4x-3=0$ has only one real solution. Hence $k>-2$, with $k\\ne-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      }
    ]
  },
